    "entire_literal_inventory_rows": [
      {
        "key": "cmdp.html::node-1138",
        "source": "SafeLearning/cmdp.html",
        "locator": "::node-1138",
        "line": 1009,
        "tag": "p",
        "classes": [],
        "source_text": "(b) Because the agent must leave $R_0$ at every step, the long-run fractions $(V_0,V_1,V_2)$ of any policy satisfy $V_0\\le\\tfrac12$, and every such point of the simplex is attained (from $R_0$ pick $R_1$ with probability $q$, stay in $R_i$ with probability $a_i$). The achievable set is thus the quadrilateral with vertices $v_1=(0,1,0)$ (stay in $R_1$), $v_2=(0,0,1)$ (stay in $R_2$), $v_3=(\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12,0)$ (alternate $R_0,R_1$), $v_4=(\\tfrac12,0,\\tfrac12)$ (alternate $R_0,R_2$). $L=V_0+\\lambda_1V_1+\\lambda_2V_2-\\tfrac{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}{3}$ is linear in the fractions, so it is maximised on a face; up to the constant $-\\tfrac{\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2}{3}$ its values at the four vertices are $\\lambda_1,\\ \\lambda_2,\\ \\tfrac{1+\\lambda_1}{2},\\ \\tfrac{1+\\lambda_2}{2}$. The optimum $(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13)$ is an interior point, so it maximises $L$ only if $L$ is constant on the whole quadrilateral, i.e. $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=1$. For $\\lambda_1\\gt\\lambda_2$ the maximal vertex is $v_1$ (if $\\lambda_1\\ge1$) or $v_3$ (if $\\lambda_1\\le1$), and every maximiser has $V_2=0$: infeasible; symmetrically for $\\lambda_2\\gt\\lambda_1$. For $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2\\lt1$ the maximisers form the edge $v_3$–$v_4$: return to $R_0$ at once, $V_1+V_2=\\tfrac12\\lt\\tfrac23$, infeasible. For $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2\\gt1$ they form the edge $v_1$–$v_2$, so every maximiser has $V_0=0$. A stationary maximiser enters one region and stays there; it is feasible in expectation when the probability $q$ of entering $R_1$ lies in $[\\tfrac13,\\tfrac23]$, while each trajectory starves one region. A history-dependent maximiser can do better on feasibility. Staying $n$ steps in $R_1$, then $n$ steps in $R_2$, for $n=1,2,\\dots$ (passing through $R_0$ once per block) visits $R_0$ only $O(\\sqrt T)$ times in $T$ steps, so it reaches $(0,\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12)$ and meets both constraints on its single trajectory. All of them are worthless, though: return $0$ against $P^\\star=\\tfrac13$. In no case is a Lagrangian maximiser optimal.",
        "text_sha256": "5d617812e199330750285b2902092609eade22e11511057acd6d4ca62505cc7c",
        "source_sha256": "bed8ea8373d33b0f33959aedb17f565c3f53c869b2714a559222caf15772e90f",
        "inventory_kind": "source_unit_needs_claim_review"
      },
      {
        "key": "cmdp.html::node-1139",
        "source": "SafeLearning/cmdp.html",
        "locator": "::node-1139",
        "line": 1010,
        "tag": "p",
        "classes": [],
        "source_text": "Why every point with $0\\lt V_0\\le\\tfrac12$ is attained: write $a_i=1-b_i$, so $b_i$ is the probability of returning from $R_i$ to $R_0$, and let $q_1=q$, $q_2=1-q$. In the long run the flow from $R_0$ into $R_i$ equals the flow back, $V_0q_i=V_ib_i$. Choosing $q_i=V_i/(1-V_0)$ and $b_i=V_0/(1-V_0)$ (which lies in $(0,1]$ exactly because $0\\lt V_0\\le\\tfrac12$) satisfies both balance equations, and the stationary fractions of this chain are then $(V_0,V_1,V_2)$; a region with $V_i=0$ is never entered. Example: $(\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13,\\tfrac13)$ needs $q=b_1=b_2=\\tfrac12$, the fair-coin policy of (a). For stationary policies, points with $V_0=0$ are attained in expectation by randomising once at the start between staying forever in $R_1$ or in $R_2$; single trajectories of that policy have fractions $(0,1,0)$ or $(0,0,1)$. History-dependent policies can also attain mixed fractions on a single trajectory, as the growing-block construction above shows.",
        "text_sha256": "0c7598de4a3bb5b7273f0e3abdf487dadbd885e4f30ce37966c7b32249c4f763",
        "source_sha256": "bed8ea8373d33b0f33959aedb17f565c3f53c869b2714a559222caf15772e90f",
        "inventory_kind": "source_unit_needs_claim_review"
      },
      {
        "key": "cmdp.html::node-1140",
        "source": "SafeLearning/cmdp.html",
        "locator": "::node-1140",
        "line": 1013,
        "tag": "p",
        "classes": [],
        "source_text": "The count behind $O(\\sqrt T)$: after the blocks of lengths $1,\\dots,N$ in both regions the agent has spent $2\\sum_{n=1}^Nn=N(N+1)$ steps in $R_1\\cup R_2$ and $2N$ steps in $R_0$, so $T\\approx N^2$ and the $R_0$ visits number about $2\\sqrt T$. A partially finished block has length at most $N+1\\approx\\sqrt T$, negligible against $T$, so the fractions in $R_1$ and $R_2$ tend to $\\tfrac12$ and that in $R_0$ to $0$.",
        "text_sha256": "e8dbe5657eb165461a0f52a4e0dd95652f17c6515c4471ed5797a3293cb1d03d",
        "source_sha256": "bed8ea8373d33b0f33959aedb17f565c3f53c869b2714a559222caf15772e90f",
        "inventory_kind": "source_unit_needs_claim_review"
      }
    ],
    "limitations": "This is the genuine growing-block trajectory component and count paragraph only. No whole Exercise8.6, dual-dynamics theorem, all-history maximizer face, stationary Markov average theorem or Saute claim is inferred."
